steps.text:
The classic model says endurance performance rests on three pillars:

- [VO2max](g:vo2max): how much oxygen you can use at most;
- how big a share of it you can hold, which tracks your [lactate threshold](g:lactate-threshold);
- economy or efficiency: how much oxygen each watt or each km costs ([gross efficiency](g:gross-efficiency), [running economy](g:running-economy)).

All three are measured in a rested athlete, so they are *start-line* values. Together they predict events like the marathon well. But they are not fixed: each can worsen as a long, tiring effort goes on [[c:dc-start-line]].

A marathon's last 10 km, the final climb of a 5 h road race, the run of an Ironman: none is done on fresh legs.
